Memory Leaks angular

- Memory leaks in an Angular application happen when objects (components, services, DOM nodes, subscriptions, etc.) 
are kept in memory longer than needed, even after the user navigates away or the component is destroyed. 
- Over time, this leads to high memory usage, slow UI, and eventual crashes, especially in long‑running SPAs.

Common Causes of Memory Leaks in Angular
1. Unsubscribed Observables
ngOnInit() {
  this.service.getData().subscribe(data => {
    this.items = data;
  });
}
If the observable is long‑lived (interval, Subject, EventEmitter, WebSocket), it keeps the component alive.

Solution:

Option1: 
Unsubscribe subscription manually.
ngOnDestroy() {
  this.subscription.unsubscribe();
}

Option2: takeUntil pattern
private destroy$ = new Subject<void>();
ngOnInit() {
  this.service.getData()
    .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$))
    .subscribe(...);
}

ngOnDestroy() {
  this.destroy$.next();
}

Option3: async pipe (BEST) 
<div *ngFor="let item of items$ | async">
-----------------------------------------------------
2. Event Listeners Not Removed
Problem:
window.addEventListener('resize', this.onResize);

Fix:

ngOnInit() {
  window.addEventListener('resize', this.onResize);
}

ngOnDestroy() {
  window.removeEventListener('resize', this.onResize);
}
OR
Prefer HostListener
@HostListener('window:resize')
onResize() {}
-----------------------------------------------------
3. SetInterval / setTimeout Not Cleared
Problem:
setInterval(() => {
	console.log("Running..");
},1000)

Fix:
ngOnDestroy() {
	clearInterval(this.intervalId);
}
-----------------------------------------------------
4. Long Living Services holding data
Services provided in root (providedIn: 'root') live for the entire app lifecycle. 
Problem: 
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class CacheService {
  data: any[] = [];
}

Solution:
Clear data when not required
clear() {
  this.data = [];
}
-----------------------------------------------------
5. Detached DOM Elements
Problem:
Occurs when DOM nodes are created dynamically
References are kept after view destruction

this.element = document.getElementById('myDiv');

Fix:
- Let Angular manage the DOM
- Avoid manual DOM manipulation
- Use Renderer2 if needed
